      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of the certificate.\n\nThis is part 4 of 4 of an OIML certificate R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12, project number 3903947, pages 11-13 of 13. It's from NMi (Netherlands).\n\nContent present in this fragment:\n1. Certificate number info: Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12, Project number 3903947, Page total 13, Member State: The Netherlands.\n2. Tables 9-12: General characteristics of the indicating device, split by:\n   - Table 9: Reduced bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 10: Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\n   - Table 11: Full bore – AC mains powered\n   - Table 12: Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered (includes continuation table on page 12 without a table label — it's the continuation of Table 11 probably; actually the unlabeled table on page 12 comes before \"Table 12\" heading, so it's likely a continuation of Table 11 Full bore – AC mains powered)\n3. Production locations: ABB Limited (UK), ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd. (China), ABB Inc. (USA), ABB India Limited (India). These are manufacturers/production locations.\n4. Certificate history / revision history: revisions Initial through 12.\n\nThe certificate is a water meter (R49), so recommendation is R49, edition 2013 (from \"R49/2013\"), scheme A (from \"-A-NL1-\"). But wait — the instruction says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present in this fragment.\"\n\nHmm, the question is whether to include the certificate-level fields. The fragment does contain the certificate number header (Number R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01 revision 12, Project number 3903947, Page 11 of 13, Member State The Netherlands). So certificate-level info IS present in the fragment. I should include it.\n\nScheme: \"A\" from \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". The scheme field expects \"A|B\".\n\nDate issued: not present in this fragment (only revision dates). Revision 12 date is 7 April 2025 — but that's a revision, not necessarily date_issued. I should not fabricate. Actually the current revision date could be the date of issue of this revision. But safer to omit date_issued since it's not explicitly stated as such. Hmm. Actually, \"revision 12\" dated 7 April 2025 — the revision history says revision 12 on 7 April 2025 \"Hardware update due to the addition of resistor alternatives.\" The certificate number includes \"revision 12\". It's reasonable to include revision_history fully and omit date_issued.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present.\n\nIssuing authority: NMi logo, Member State The Netherlands. Name \"NMi\" — can I infer? The logo says \"[Logo: NMi]\". The issuing authority name is NMi (Nederlands Meetinstituut). I could include issuing_authority with name \"NMi\". But address etc. not present. Hmm, should I include it? The fragment has the NMi logo and OIML Member State: The Netherlands. I think a minimal issuing_authority with name \"NMi\" is acceptable. Or I could omit. The instruction says omit sections not present. The NMi logo is present. I'll include name: \"NMi\" and member_state via certificate.member_state: \"The Netherlands\".\n\nApplicants: not present in this fragment (probably on earlier pages). Production locations are listed — these are manufacturers/production locations. Should I include them as manufacturers[]? The section says \"Production location — The water meter is produced at one of the following production locations\". These are manufacturing locations. I'll include them under manufacturers[] (or maybe a note). The schema has manufacturers[] with name and address_lines. I'll include the four ABB entities.\n\nCertified type: category \"Water meter\" (R49 is water meters). Type designations not in this fragment. Model family: the tables reference meter sizes DN40...DN1800 and configurations (reduced bore / full bore, AC mains / battery-renewable). Should I populate model_family? This fragment only covers indicating device characteristics by meter size. The models here are DN sizes. But the certificate covers many DN sizes across bore types and power sources. Given the fragment, I could emit matrix_tables for Tables 9-12 with config axes.\n\nThe tables are 2-D: rows = meter size groups, columns = indicating range max, indicating range min, verification scale interval max. Plus the config axes: bore (reduced/full) and power source (AC mains / battery-renewable).\n\nBest representation: matrix_tables[] with one entry per table, including the config in the name/description. Also could populate characteristics.config_level with axis \"power_supply\" and \"bore\" — but that might be complex. Let me think.\n\nRule 5: Configuration axes — when attributes vary by configuration, use characteristics.config_level with axis describing the dimension.\n\nHere we have two axes: bore (reduced/full) and power (AC mains / battery-renewable). Attributes: indicating_range_max, indicating_range_min, verification_scale_interval_max, each varying by meter size within each config combination.\n\nI could emit config_level entries:\n- attribute: \"indicating_range_maximum\", axis: \"bore_and_power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"reduced bore, AC mains powered, DN40-DN65\", value: 9999999}, ...] — this gets messy because value also depends on meter size.\n\nProbably cleaner: matrix_tables for each of the 4 tables (plus the continuation table on page 12), and let model_family/models include DN sizes? Hmm.\n\nActually, the model family for this certificate: the models are meter sizes DN40 through DN1800 with bore variants. But this fragment doesn't state the full model list; it references meter sizes within tables. I think populating model_family from this fragment is risky since the full model list with type designations is presumably in earlier fragments. The instruction says extract content present in THIS fragment only.\n\nI'll include:\n- certificate: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, revision (12)\n- issuing_authority: name \"NMi\" (from logo), member state... Actually schema for issuing_authority doesn't include member_state; it's on certificate. I'll include just name. Hmm, or omit issuing_authority entirely? The logo is \"[Logo: NMi]\" — that indicates NMi is the issuer. I'll include name: \"NMi\".\n\n- manufacturers[]: the four production locations. Actually, should production locations be manufacturers? They produce the water meter, so yes. Alternatively could add role info via characteristics? The schema doesn't have role for manufacturers. I'll just list them.\n\n- matrix_tables[]: 5 tables (9, 10, 11, 11-continuation, 12).\n\nLet me structure each matrix table:\n\nTable 9: \"General characteristics of the indicating device — Reduced bore – AC mains powered\"\ncolumns: meter_size, indicating_range_maximum, indicating_range_minimum, verification_scale_interval_maximum\ncolumn_units: [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"]\nrows:\n- {meter_size: [\"DN40\",\"DN50\",\"DN65\"] or \"DN40, DN50, DN65\"?} — I'll keep the grouped condition string: \"DN40, DN50, DN65\". Values: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001 (converted from 0,0001).\n- {meter_size: \"DN80\", max: 9999999, min: 999999, vsi: 0.0001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200\", max: 9999999, min: 999999, vsi: 0.001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN250\", max: 9999999, min: 9999999, vsi: 0.001}\n- {meter_size: \"DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600\", max: 9999999, min: 9999999, vsi: 0.01}\n\nTable 10: \"Reduced bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\"\n- {DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001}\n- {DN65: 9999999, 99999, 0.001}\n- {DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001}\n- {DN200: 9999999, 999999, 0.01}\n- {DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\n- {DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n\nTable 11: \"Full bore – AC mains powered\"\n- {DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.0001}\n- {DN65: 9999999, 999999, 0.0001}\n- {DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.001}\n- {DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\nContinuation (page 12, unlabeled — continuation of Table 11):\n- {DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n- {DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1}\n- {DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999, 99999999, 1}\n\nWait — the unlabeled table on page 12 appears before \"Table 12\" heading. So it's the continuation of Table 11 (Full bore – AC mains powered). Yes, since Table 11 lists full bore AC mains up to DN300 on page 11, and the page-12 table continues with DN350-DN1800. I'll represent it as \"Table 11 (continued)\".\n\nTable 12: \"Full bore – Battery / Renewable energy powered\"\n- {DN40, DN50: 9999999, 99999, 0.001}\n- {DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999, 999999, 0.001}\n- {DN150: 9999999, 999999, 0.01}\n- {DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999, 9999999, 0.01}\n- {DN600: 99999999, 9999999, 0.1}\n- {DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999, 99999999, 0.1}\n- {DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999, 99999999, 1}\n\nShould I also populate characteristics.config_level? I could add config_level entries for verification_scale_interval etc., but that would duplicate matrix tables heavily. The schema says matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. These tables vary by both meter size (model-ish axis) and config. I think matrix_tables is the right home; maybe also add config_level for the indicating device characteristics with axis combining bore and power... That's messy. I'll include matrix_tables and optionally a light config_level? I think I'll skip config_level to avoid duplication and noise, and rely on matrix_tables. Hmm, but rule 5 says use config_level when attributes vary by configuration. Here the tables are organized by configuration (bore × power) and rows are meter sizes. The meter size axis is more like a model axis.\n\nAlternatively I could populate model_family.models with each DN size? That would be 20+ DN sizes × ... but the fragment alone references many sizes. I think the cleanest approach consistent with the schema: matrix_tables (4-5 entries), and optionally config_level entries capturing the axes. Let me consider adding config_level:\n\nattribute: \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\", axis: \"bore_type_and_power_supply\", values: [{condition: \"Reduced bore, AC mains, DN40-DN65\", value: 0.0001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, ...]. This is very redundant. I'll skip config_level and keep matrix_tables with names embedding the config.\n\nActually, maybe I could add config_level with attribute \"indicating_device_characteristics\" pointing to tables? No — keep it clean.\n\n- recommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\". From certificate number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Accuracy classes: not in this fragment (revision 6 mentions \"accuracy class 1\" addition, but accuracy_classes for recommendation — revision 6 says \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800\" and revision 3 mentions \"E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter\". Hmm, those are mentioned in revision descriptions. Should I include accuracy_classes: [1, \"E2\", \"O\"]? That's from revision descriptions which mention classes. R49 uses accuracy classes 1 and 2 (water meters), and E2/O... actually E2, O... those might be electromagnetic environment classes (E1, E2, O...) per R49. Hmm, R49 has accuracy classes 1 and 2, and environment classes E1/E2 and O/M... Actually R49-1 defines ambient classes... The revision notes mention \"E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter\" — these could be humidity/electromagnetic classes. Including accuracy_classes: [1] seems plausible but risky. Revision 6: \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\" Accuracy class 1 is an R49 accuracy class. I could include accuracy_classes: [\"1\"] or [1]. Hmm. I think it's defensible but the fragment doesn't clearly state the certified accuracy classes overall. I'll omit accuracy_classes to avoid fabrication. Or include [1]? The mention in revision history suggests class 1 was added; class 2 presumably was there from the start. I'd rather not guess. Omit.\n\n- test_reports: none in fragment.\n- footnotes: none.\n- revision_history: from the table:\n  - revision \"Initial\", date \"14 August 2020\" → ISO \"2020-08-14\", changes \"First issue.\"\n  - \"1\", \"2020-09-30\", \"Additional evaluation of reduced bore larger sizes up to DN600.\"\n  - \"2\", \"2020-12-24\", \"Additional evaluation of (virtual) full bore sensor and additional software version\"\n  - \"3\", \"2021-08-02\", \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to –25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\"\n  - \"4\", \"2021-08-26\", \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1200.\"\n  - \"5\", \"2021-11-23\", \"Addition of full-bore sizes up to DN1800.\"\n  - \"6\", \"2022-05-20\", \"Addition of accuracy class 1 to DN1800.\"\n  - \"7\", \"2022-08-30\", \"Software version updates due to addition of microprocessors.\"\n  - \"8\", \"2023-07-03\", \"Software update\"\n  - \"9\", \"2023-09-23\", \"Addition of AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device. Software update\"\n  - \"10\", \"2024-02-26\", \"Software update and change to U200 wireless module for global use.\"\n  - \"11\", \"2025-01-27\", \"Software update.\"\n  - \"12\", \"2025-04-07\", \"Hardware update due to the addition of resistor alternatives.\"\n\nRevision format: \"revision (string, e.g. \"0\" not 0)\". For \"Initial\" I'll use \"Initial\" as string. Dates: I'll use ISO format \"2020-08-14\". Or keep original text? ISO is cleaner and parseable. I'll use ISO.\n\n- certificate: \n  - number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — include revision? The number field: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\". Revision separate — schema doesn't have revision field in certificate, but revision_history covers it. There's no \"revision\" field at certificate level in the schema. Hmm — I could include number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" and rely on revision_history for rev 12. Or include number with revision... The schema: number, scheme, project_number, page_total, member_state, date_issued, oiml_issuer_id. I'll set number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\", scheme: \"A\", project_number: \"3903947\", page_total: 13, member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued: omit (not explicitly stated; could infer 2025-04-07 for rev 12 but don't fabricate). Hmm, actually, is it fabricating to say date_issued is 2025-04-07? The current revision 12 is dated 7 April 2025, and this document is revision 12. It's a reasonable inference but the fragment doesn't say \"date of issue\". I'll omit to be safe.\n\n  - scheme: from \"A\" in the number.\n\n- certified_type: category \"Water meter\". The fragment mentions \"The water meter is produced at...\" — so category is Water meter. Type designations: revision 9 mentions \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" — that's a variant of the calculating and indicating device. Is that a type designation present in the fragment? \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant of calculating and indicating device\" — yes, that names a type. I could include certified_type.type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]? Hmm, that's from revision history describing what was added at revision 9. It does identify a certified type component. Given the fragment otherwise lacks type designations, including this from revision history is a judgment call. I think it's OK to include \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" as a type designation mentioned. But careful: \"FEW45Y.Z\" — the text is \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant\". I'll include it. Actually, maybe safer to include under components with role \"calculating and indicating device\"? The schema says components[] include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies. Revision 9 names \"calculating and indicating device\" variant. Hmm, that's a revision description, not a current spec statement. I'll keep it simple: include certified_type with category \"Water meter\" and maybe type_designations [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"]. Hmm.\n\nLet me reconsider: The instruction says \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only. Omit sections that are not present.\" The fragment's core content: header info, tables 9-12, production locations, revision history. I'll include:\n\n- certificate\n- issuing_authority (name NMi from logo)\n- manufacturers (4 production locations)\n- certified_type (category: \"Water meter\")\n- matrix_tables (5 entries)\n- recommendation (R49, 2013, scheme A)\n- revision_history (13 entries)\n\nShould I include model_family? The models here are DN sizes... I think matrix_tables suffice for this fragment. The full model family is presumably in earlier fragments. To be helpful, I might add model_family with models per DN size? That would be 25+ models, and each config combination changes characteristics. It's overkill for this fragment; the instruction for R137 example shows model_family populated from a size table. Here the size axis exists but crossed with config axes. I'll rely on matrix_tables and skip model_family. Hmm, but the guidance emphasizes matrix detection as MOST IMPORTANT. The tables ARE captured in matrix_tables with full fidelity. I think that's fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider adding characteristics.model_level for meter sizes: attribute \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\" values per DN... but values differ by config (4 combos), so a single model_level per attribute can't capture it without model IDs like \"DN40/reduced/AC\". Too convoluted. matrix_tables it is.\n\nNow the unlabeled table on page 12: I'll name it \"Table 11 (continued) — Full bore – AC mains powered\" with description noting it continues Table 11 from page 12.\n\nManufacturers addresses:\n- ABB Limited: \"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK).\" → address_lines: [\"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\"]. I'll drop trailing period.\n- ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.: [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\"]\n- ABB Inc.: [\"125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\"]\n- ABB India Limited: [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\"]\n\nIssuing authority name: \"NMi\" — from logo. I could write \"NMi (Netherlands Meetsinstituut)\"? No, don't fabricate. Just \"NMi\".\n\nCertified type: category \"Water meter\". Include description? Not needed.\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\". accuracy_classes: omit... Actually revision 6 explicitly mentions accuracy class 1. And R49 water meters have class 1 and 2. The fragment only evidences class 1. I'll omit accuracy_classes since incomplete. Hmm, or include [\"1\"]? \"Don't fabricate\" — including only class 1 would misrepresent (class 2 likely also covered from initial). Omit.\n\nLet me also double check: should certificate.number include revision? I'll use \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\" — clean.\n\nPage_total: 13.\n\nNow write matrix tables JSON:\n\nTable 9:\n{\n  \"name\": \"Table 9 — General characteristics of the indicating device (Reduced bore – AC mains powered)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"indicating_range_maximum\", \"indicating_range_minimum\", \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\"],\n  \"column_units\": [\"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\", \"m³\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN40, DN50, DN65\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 99999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.0001},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN80\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.0001},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN100, DN125, DN150, DN200\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN250\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.001},\n    {\"meter_size\": \"DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600\", \"indicating_range_maximum\": 9999999, \"indicating_range_minimum\": 9999999, \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\": 0.01}\n  ],\n  \"description\": \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\"\n}\n\nTable 10:\nrows:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999 / 99999 / 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999 / 99999 / 0.001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.001\n- DN200: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.01\n- DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.01\n- DN500, DN600: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.1\n\nTable 11:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999 / 99999 / 0.0001\n- DN65: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.0001\n- DN80, DN100, DN125, DN150: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.001\n- DN200, DN250, DN300: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.01\n\nTable 11 continued (page 12):\n- DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500, DN600: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800, DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200: 99999999 / 99999999 / 0.1\n- DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650, DN1800: 999999999 / 99999999 / 1\n\nTable 12:\n- DN40, DN50: 9999999 / 99999 / 0.001\n- DN65, DN80, DN100, DN125: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.001\n- DN150: 9999999 / 999999 / 0.01\n- DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500: 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.01\n- DN600: 99999999 / 9999999 / 0.1\n- DN700, DN750, DN800: 99999999 / 99999999 / 0.1\n- DN900, DN1000, DN1050, DN1100, DN1200, DN1350, DN1400, DN1500, DN1600, DN1650: 999999999 / 99999999 / 1\n\nNote the \"1\" values — dimensionless counts of m³, unit m³ for all these. The VSI value \"1\" with unit m³ — value: 1, but unit in column_units.\n\nDo I need footnote_markers in matrix rows? No footnotes present.\n\nShould matrix rows values be structured objects? The schema example for matrix_tables rows shows plain values ({\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\": 4.0, ...}). So plain values are fine for matrix_tables.\n\nShould I also add characteristics.config_level? Let me add a modest set capturing the config axes for indicating device characteristics? I keep going back and forth. Given rule 5 and the emphasis, maybe I should include config_level with axis \"bore_and_power_supply\" for \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\" etc. But each value also depends on DN size — the condition would need to include DN ranges. E.g.:\n\n{\"attribute\": \"verification_scale_interval_maximum\", \"axis\": \"bore_type_and_power_supply\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"Reduced bore, AC mains powered, DN40–DN65\", \"value\": 0.0001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  ...\n]}\n\nThat's ~20 entries across 3 attributes × 4 configs. Very redundant with matrix_tables. I'll skip config_level; matrix_tables is explicitly for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\" — this fits that description (attribute × DN-size × 2 config axes = 3-D, can't be flattened into config_level cleanly).\n\nAlso \"Certificate history: This revision replaces the previous version.\" — could note in revision history; not needed.\n\nProduction location section: should I put these under manufacturers? The schema manufacturers[] has name, address_lines. Yes. But note: production locations may differ from the \"Manufacturer\" listed earlier. Still, in this fragment they're the only parties. I'll include them as manufacturers with a note? No note field. Fine.\n\ncertified_type: category \"Water meter\". Should I include module_designation? Not present. Omit.\n\nShould I include components for \"calculating and indicating device\" and \"flow transmitter\" mentioned in revision history? Those are mentions in revision descriptions, not current component specs. I'll skip.\n\nOne more consideration: certificate.scheme — the number contains \"-A-\" indicating Scheme A (single-stage). Yes, scheme: \"A\".\n\noiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\nLet me now also decide about \"issuing_authority\". The logo \"[Logo: NMi]\" — I'll include name \"NMi\". Some might argue omit since it's just a logo marker. But it's reasonable. I'll include it with name \"NMi\".\n\nAlso should I include certificate member_state \"The Netherlands\" — yes.\n\nFinal JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"3903947\",\n    \"page_total\": 13,\n    \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"NMi\"\n  },\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\"name\": \"ABB Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Oldends Lane, GL10 3TA Stonehouse, United Kingdom (UK)\"]},\n    {\"name\": \"ABB Engineering (Shanghai) Ltd.\", \"address_lines\": [\"No. 4528, Kangxin Highway, Pudong New District, Shanghai, 201319, P.R. China\"]},\n    {\"name\": \"ABB Inc.\", \"address_lines\": [\"125 E. County Line Road, Warminster, PA 18974, United States of America (USA)\"]},\n    {\"name\": \"ABB India Limited\", \"address_lines\": [\"Plot No. 5 & 6, 2nd Phase, Peenya Industrial Area, Bengaluru – 560058, Karnataka India\"]}\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\"\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ ... 5 entries ... ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\"\n  },\n  \"revision_history\": [ ...13 entries... ]\n}\n\nWait — should type_designations include \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"? It's mentioned in revision 9 as a variant of the calculating and indicating device. Given \"certified_type.type_designations: all type/model identifiers mentioned\" — it IS mentioned in this fragment. I'll include it. Hmm, \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\" — the exact string \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z Mobile comms variant\". The type designation is \"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\". I'll include that. Actually this could help downstream merging. Yes, include type_designations: [\"AquaMaster4 FEW45Y.Z\"].\n\nDates in revision history: use ISO format. Revision \"Initial\" → keep \"Initial\".\n\nRevision 3 changes text: \"Additional evaluation for extending temperature to – 25 °C and for E2 class and O class for the flow transmitter. Addition of software version.\" — keep as-is (fix the weird spacing: \"–25 °C\"). I'll write \"extending temperature to −25 °C\". Keep en-dash/minus as in original: \"– 25 °C\" → \"–25 °C\". Fine.\n\nNow, about the numbers: 9999999 etc. — integers, fine. VSI values 0.0001 etc.\n\nDouble-check Table 10 row \"DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450\": 9999999, 9999999, 0.01. Yes.\n\nTable 12 \"DN200, DN250, DN300, DN350, DN400, DN450, DN500\": 9999999 / 9999999 / 0.01. Yes.\n\nTable 12 \"DN600\": 99999999 / 9999999 / 0.1. Yes.\n\nDescription fields for matrix_tables: include the config label, e.g. \"Reduced bore – AC mains powered\". For the continued table, description: \"Continuation of Table 11 (Full bore – AC mains powered) on page 12.\"\n\nNames: \"Table 9 – Indicating device characteristics (Reduced bore, AC mains powered)\" etc.\n\nOne concern: en-dashes in names are fine in JSON.\n\nShould matrix_tables include \"footnotes\": []? Omit (empty). \n\nAlso maybe include config_level after all? I'll decide: skip. matrix_tables fully preserve the data.\n\nOutput only JSON.",
